// article flow(2)

## Question

How can one document be pleasant to read in a browser while carrying enough
structure for a later PDF renderer? The content records meaning; the header
chooses a serif face, a measured line, and line spacing for this project.
The `flow(2)` group begins after the abstract and contents, so both remain
full-width while the report body uses two columns.

// context aside()

The `aside` alias expands to `skinny(72%) color(muted)`. It is reusable across
documents without repeating the command chain.

// end context

## Method

// options comparison()

### Source

Write headings, prose, and command groups in the source document.
Keep the section order explicit. A renderer may change line breaks, but it
must preserve the order of headings, paragraphs, figures, and references.

// --

### Presentation

Keep page typography in the shared `.sxh` header.
The header is loaded by `strike.yaml`, then layered with any site header.
Aliases are resolved before either renderer walks the document tree.

// end options

### A compact code explanation

```zig
const result = try render(source, .{ .sheet = header });
```

// explanation code-note()

The call passes the same header to every document in the project. The
`code-note` alias gives the explanation a consistent muted treatment.
The source line is an example of where configuration enters the pipeline;
neither renderer needs to parse typography out of prose.

// end explanation

## Result

The table of contents, comparison, aside, and code explanation remain ordinary
document structure. Their presentation can change without changing the prose.
The body moves from the first column to the second in source order. A longer
version continues on the next physical page in PDF; HTML keeps the same
ordered content in a column container.

## Discussion

Two renderers need different measurement engines. HTML delegates line and
column breaks to CSS. The native PDF backend measures in points and advances
to another column when the remaining height is too small. Both receive the
same parsed headings, paragraphs, code, and groups. This distinction matters:
the document states *what* flows, while each backend decides *where* a line
break occurs.

The template also shows a current limitation. A wide figure inside the body
has no accepted span command yet. Place full-width material before or after
the flow group, or use a narrower figure that fits one column. A later design
decision can add a span form without changing this document's reading order.

## Conclusion

A report template is useful when the source remains ordinary enough to edit
without a layout tool. This one uses headings for navigation, a header for
typographic defaults, aliases for recurring components, and one flow group
for the main body. Each piece has a clear place in the source. That makes the
document easier to revise: changing the theme does not move paragraphs, and
rewording a section does not require manually reassigning it to a column.

// end article
